INT. FUNICULAR RAILWAY - MOUNTAIN CAR - NIGHT

A wooden cabin on rails, climbing a slope so steep the benches are stepped like stairs. Lantern light. Black pines slide by the windows.

ROSAMUND ACHTERBERG (30s, conductor's cap, a brass ticket punch) stands at the front. NICOLAI VELASQUEZ-BRANDT (40s, a long coat, a leather case chained to his wrist) sits at the back.

ROSAMUND
Tickets, sir.

NICOLAI
I've paid.

ROSAMUND
Then show me.

He holds up a stub. She punches it. Their eyes meet. She looks at the case.

ROSAMUND (CONT'D)
That's a lot to carry up a mountain.

NICOLAI
It's a lot to carry anywhere.

The car lurches. Both grab rails. The lantern swings, throwing shadows. A second beat, then the car steadies.

ROSAMUND
Cable's tired. It does that.

NICOLAI
Does it?

ROSAMUND
Every night.

He releases the rail. She does not. She edges closer.

ROSAMUND (CONT'D)
I've been told to watch for a man with a case.

NICOLAI
Told by whom?

ROSAMUND
By a man who says he wants it back.

They both lunge. The punch clatters across the floor. The case skids. He kicks it under a bench. She seizes his sleeve. He twists free. The car pitches and both go sprawling.

Rosamund scrambles to the brake lever. Nicolai grabs her ankle. She kicks him off, hauls the lever. The car shudders, groans, and stops dead.

Silence. Pine needles tick against the glass.

NICOLAI
(panting)
That was clever.

ROSAMUND
(panting)
I know.

NICOLAI
Now what?

ROSAMUND
Now we wait for the man who wants it.

NICOLAI
You'd rather I hand it over to him?

ROSAMUND
I'd rather live.

A pause. He slides the case out from under the bench. Unlocks the chain. Holds it out.

NICOLAI
Then you keep it.

She doesn't take it. A light bobs in the pines below. Voices.

ROSAMUND
They're early.

NICOLAI
They were always early.

She snatches the case, yanks open the rear door, and shoves him through it into the dark. He drops two meters to the slope and rolls. She follows, case in her arms, and they run side by side up the mountain through the trees.
